usb: ipheth: kill in-flight URBs on close, freeing the endpoint

ipheth_close() stops the queue and cancels the carrier work but never
drops the URBs it has posted, so an administratively down interface
keeps its RX URB in flight and the endpoint claimed. Only unbind or
disconnect -- which reach ipheth_kill_urbs() -- release it.

That is a leak in its own right: an RX URB outliving the netdev being
down. On the F1C200s it also blocks wired CarPlay outright. The MUSB
host cannot service the iPhone's usbmux bulk-IN endpoint (EP 0x85) while
ipheth holds an RX URB on the same device; a standalone usbfs probe with
the phone in USB config 6 and usb0 up gets no mux VERSION reply at all
with ipheth bound, and replies reproducibly after
"echo 1-1:6.2 > /sys/bus/usb/drivers/ipheth/unbind". Rebinding kills it
again. cdc_ncm's usb0 can stay up throughout, so it is specifically
ipheth, not NCM traffic.

ipheth does not use the usbnet framework -- it manages a single RX URB
itself, submitted in ipheth_open() and resubmitted every second by
ipheth_carrier_check_work(). So capping usbnet's rx_qlen does nothing
here, and "ip link set <if> down" is currently not enough either.

Reuse the existing ipheth_kill_urbs() helper rather than open-coding.
Order matters: the carrier work is what resubmits RX, so it must be
cancelled first, which the existing code already does. ipheth_open()
resubmits, so bringing the interface back up still works.

With this, taking the interface down is sufficient to free the endpoint
-- no driver unbinding, and nothing that has to re-fire each time the
phone re-enumerates on the config-6 switch.

Not yet built or tested on hardware. The decisive check is a mux
VERSION reply, or non-zero musb_urb_rx completions on the mux IN
endpoint, while usb0 is up and ipheth's interface is down.

--- a/drivers/net/usb/ipheth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/usb/ipheth.c
@@ -500,6 +500,13 @@
 
 	netif_stop_queue(net);
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&dev->carrier_work);
+	/*
+	 * Drop the URBs the interface had in flight. Without this the RX URB
+	 * stays posted while the netdev is down, keeping the endpoint claimed;
+	 * ipheth_open() resubmits, so reopening still works. Order matters --
+	 * the carrier work is what resubmits RX, so it must be cancelled first.
+	 */
+	ipheth_kill_urbs(dev);
 	return 0;
 }
 
